# Reverse an array (using extra space)

##Problem Statement 
We need to reverse all the elements of array. 

##Approach
Start the loop backwards (traversal is from the index : n-1) and print the elements of array to index zero.

##Algorithm
1. Start
2. Read an array of size 'n' and its elements.
3. Apply a for loop and traverse the elements from the back i.e. idx = (n-1).
4. Print all the elements of array to get the required reverse of array.
5. End.

## C++ Implementation --- done in two ways : 1. By using extra space.
                                             2. By without using extra spaces. 
------------------ BY USING EXTRA SPACES---------------
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

void print(int *arr, int n){
  for(int i=0; i<n; i++){
    cout << arr[i];
  }
cout << endl;
}

int main(){
int arr[] = {5,4,3,9,2,6};
int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(int);
int copyArr[n];
for(int i=0; i<n; i++){
  int j = n-i-1;
  copyArr[i] = arr[j];
}
for(int i=0; i<n; i++){
  arr[i] = copyArr[i];
}
print(arr , n);
return 0;
}


-----------WITHOUT USING EXTRA SPACES------------
#include<iostream>
#include<math.h>
using namespace std;

void print(int *arr, int n){
for(int i=0; i<n; i++){
  cout << arr[i] << " " ; 
  }
cout << endl;
}

int main(){
int arr[] = {5,4,3,9,2,6};
int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(int);
int start = 0; 
int end = (n-1);
while(start < end){
  swap(arr[start] , arr[end]);
  start++;
  end--;
}
print(arr, n);
return 0;
}


##Time Complexity
by using extra spaces : 0(n)
without using extra spaces: 0(n)

##Space Complexity
by using extra spaces : 0(n)
without using extra spaces : 0(1)
